}
\description{
Computes the asymptotic variance of the sample coefficient alpha
(Guttman, 1945; Cronbach, 1951) under multivariate normality of the
\emph{p} item scores, using the closed form derived by van Zyl,
Neudecker, & Nel (2000) under the assumption that the population
covariance matrix has equal off-diagonals (the parallel-items model),
along with the simpler Bonett (2002) approximation
\eqn{2p/(p-1) \cdot (1 - \alpha)^2 / (n - 1)} that is widely used in
planning.
}
\details{
Companion to the existing reliability-coefficient infrastructure
(\code{\link{reliability_alpha}}, \code{\link{reliability}}) and a
building block for AIPE planning around alpha.


\strong{van Zyl-Neudecker-Nel (2000) variance.} Under multivariate
normality and the parallel-items model (all items have equal
variances and equal pairwise covariances), the asymptotic variance of
the maximum likelihood estimator of alpha is
\deqn{\mathrm{Var}(\hat\alpha) \;=\;
  \frac{2 p (1 - \alpha)^2}{(p - 1)(n - 2)}.}
This is one of two closed forms van Zyl et al. derive; the more
general (non-parallel) form involves matrix expressions and is
implemented separately by the existing reliability infrastructure.

\strong{Bonett (2002) approximation.} Bonett (2002) gives the easy
planning form
\deqn{\mathrm{Var}(\hat\alpha) \;\approx\;
  \frac{2 p}{(p - 1)} \cdot \frac{(1 - \alpha)^2}{n - 1}.}
This differs from the van Zyl form only in the denominator (\eqn{n-1}
vs.\ \eqn{n-2}) and converges to the same value for moderate \eqn{n}.
Bonett's version is what most sample size tables use.

\strong{When to use which.} For inference (a CI on \eqn{\alpha}), the
van Zyl form is preferable, especially at small \eqn{n}; for
sample size \emph{planning} the difference is immaterial and the
Bonett form is widely cited and easier to invert.
}
